.\" Made from the kstar help (src/help.rs); do not edit. See src/manual.rs.
.TH KSTAR-UPDATE 1 "" "Kleene Star" "Kleene Star Manual"
.SH NAME
kstar\-update \- update kstar and its VS Code extension
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBkstar update\fR [\-\-check] [\-\-yes] [\-\-no-vscode]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Asks GitHub for the latest stable release (through the \fBgh\fR command, or
\fBcurl\fR with GITHUB_TOKEN). If it is newer than this kstar, it shows both
versions and what the release is about, and asks "Update to vX.Y.Z?
[Y/n]". On yes it installs the release with its install script into the
folder kstar is in, which also updates the VS Code extension; on no it
changes nothing. Pre-releases (such as 0.2.5-beta) are not offered: install
one by name with KSTAR_VERSION (see docs/install.md).
.PP
When standard input is not a terminal (a script or CI), it does not ask and
does not update: pass \-\-yes.
.PP
If VS Code is installed (the \fBcode\fR command) and kstar is up to date, it
also makes sure the Kleene Star extension matches this kstar: when it is
missing or from another release, it downloads kleene-star-<version>.vsix
from this kstar's release, checks its SHA-256 checksum and installs it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR
only say whether a newer release exists, and whether the VS Code extension matches, without installing anything
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
update without asking
.TP
\fB\-\-no-vscode\fR
leave VS Code alone (so does KSTAR_NO_VSCODE=1)
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
\fBkstar update \-\-check\fR
see whether there is a newer release
.TP
\fBkstar update\fR
update, after asking
.TP
\fBkstar update \-\-yes \-\-no-vscode\fR
update without asking, leaving VS Code alone
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBkstar\fR(1),
\fBkstar\-help\fR(1)
.PP
The reference and the tutorial: https://github.com/Pratham71/kleene\-star
